So far, integrating AI-assisted learning into my project has highlighted several practical challenges, particularly around sensor mounting.
The main difficulty lies in securely and non-invasively mounting the sensors on the dirt bike. Ideally, I would avoid drilling any holes in the engine block, oil pan, or radiator for several important reasons: preserving the structural integrity of the components, preventing potential leaks or contamination, maintaining warranty/resale value, and minimizing risk of mechanical failure during aggressive off-road use.
For the oil level sensor (ultrasonic), placing it inside the oil pan is not feasible without major disassembly or modification. One promising alternative would be to integrate the sensor into a custom or modified oil fill cap — allowing non-invasive measurement from the top — but I currently lack the tools, materials, or fabrication capabilities to prototype this effectively.
The coolant level sensor faces similar constraints: accessing the radiator or overflow reservoir without drilling or permanent alterations is challenging, and the same fabrication limitations apply.
These mounting issues represent the biggest real-world barrier to turning the theoretical IoT design into a reliable, rider-friendly prototype on an actual dirt bike.
The air pressure sensor can be mounted more easily than the oil or coolant level sensors and does not need direct access inside sealed components. However, the sensor board itself must be protected from the elements to prevent water ingress, corrosion, or damage from debris.
For a functional prototype, the goal is to prove the concept works so in the sens of the bike sitting there and taking it on and off and not fully riding with it on. 
